00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:22.080
Does it make everyone look uniform?

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:23.360
Is it a uniform?

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:25.440
-No.
-I just realised what the word means...

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:28.040
-Oh yeah.
-That's what uniform means.

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:29.280
Oh yeah.

00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:31.960
We all learned something today.

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:33.840
Well at least we did.

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:35.440
Yeah, it taught me a lesson.

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:38.480
The leading members of the society
were Lady Harberton,

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:41.280
Mary Eliza Haweis
, I'm probably mispronouncing that,

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:44.280
and Constance Wilde, the wife of Oscar Wilde.

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:47.960
Now, given we're in 1881 and those are…

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:50.080
Oh, egalitarian standards of dress.

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:52.320
Egalitarian in what sense?

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:54.080
Gender equality.

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:59.880
Yes, I'll give you the point but it's very
specifically something about women's dress then,

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:01.440
that men's dress didn't do.

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:04.040
Oh, is it egalitarian as in like
made out of eagles?

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:10.280
I realise I'm asking three blokes here
who are not from the 1880s but…

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:11.680
Speak for yourself.

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:17.760
Men's dress had certain freedoms
that women's dress didn't back then.

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:19.800
Is it pockets and the ability
to ride a bicycle?

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:21.320
Ooh, I'll give you a point for bicycle

00:13:21.320 --> 00:13:25.240
because the Lady Cyclists Association
were very keen on this.

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:28.040
Because ladies were encouraged
to ride side-saddle.

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:30.200
Now imagine how that worked, everybody,

00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:33.520
in that they could only kind of
go on one pedal basically,

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:35.240
because skirts.

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:39.720
Is it going back to the big flow-y skirts
and corsetry

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:41.840
and all of that kind of stuff
that they all wore?

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:43.720
What about corsetry?

00:13:43.720 --> 00:13:45.080
It was right hurty.

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:50.360
Yes. They were asking for freedom of movement
and absence of pressure.

00:13:50.360 --> 00:13:51.760
-Oh!
-I don't blame them.

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:54.160
The last one then in clothing controversies.

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:56.480
What is vanity sizing?

00:13:56.480 --> 00:13:57.920
Oh!

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:04.000
Again, I heard all the women in the audience
going 'yeah, we know about this'.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.720
We’ve got three blokes from Yorkshire here,
good luck.

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:10.840
If you can imagine what it would look like
for three monkeys scrapping in a barrel of tar,

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:12.520
this is what it's going to look like right now.

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:17.280
It is where the actual measured dimension
of any given garment of you,

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:20.000
is quite different to the size on the label.

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:25.560
You know how we...
we knew the things about knitting earlier?

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:28.240
-Yeah.
-We might know some things about clothing.

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:31.640
-Gary?
-Yeah, the wife crochets and knits, so...

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:37.640
Lovely. In which case at the end of the show,
congratulations, you all win this one.

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:39.000
Hey!

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:41.000
Cop-out! Cop-out!

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:42.920
I refuse to win.

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:44.760
Fine, Gary and Matt! You win this one.

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:46.000
Hey!

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:50.000
Well, I think the winner is the audience.

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:50.860
Aww.

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:51.800
Liar!

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:54.520
No, the real winning is in your hearts.

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:55.840
Jesus, f***.

00:14:57.280 --> 00:14:58.800
You are a knob.

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:02.480
Congratulations, you win a DVD
of the little known crossover

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:06.440
between a marsupial from Australia
and a Time Lord from Gallifrey.

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:08.160
It's Skippy the Bush Dr Who.

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:09.600
Hey!

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:13.720
With that for the final time we say goodbye to
Chris Joel,

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:16.360
-Gary Brannan.
-I don’t want to go.

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:18.160
-Matt Gray.
-Bye.

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:20.160
I've been Tom Scott, we'll see you around.

